WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:08.230
Now that we know a little bit more about Chaos as a whole, the Chaos Editor, and what it can do with clothing,

2
00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:15.230
let's get to the hands-on portion and look at creating our very first piece of Chaos clothing.

3
00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:19.230
And to do this, what we're going to be doing is we are going to be creating a flag.

4
00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.230
So along with this tutorial, you should have received some files.

5
00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:30.230
They should be four FBXs, one called flag and then flag LOD 1, 2, and 3.

6
00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.229
We are going to use these to create a flag.

7
00:00:33.229 --> 00:00:38.229
Now these were created inside of a digital content creation program called 3D Studio Max.

8
00:00:38.229 --> 00:00:46.229
They were then exported with the FBX format using smoothing groups, tangents, and binormals, and triangulate, all enabled.

9
00:00:46.229 --> 00:00:54.649
Then once we have all of those ready to go inside of Unreal, we imported just the flag.fbx file.

10
00:00:54.649 --> 00:01:01.649
Once we have all of that imported, we are then ready to activate cloth paint and get everything set up.

11
00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:08.640
However, there's one other thing I would like to bring up about this particular tutorial.

12
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.640
We're doing this to make this easy to understand and easy to use.

13
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.640
So we picked an easy asset, which would be just a simple plane to represent a flag.

14
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:24.640
However, in production, this is going to be a very, very high performance option

15
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:32.640
for something that should be regulated to something like a GPU simulation using vertex position offset or something like that.

16
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.640
So we are using something here to show you the basic operation of it.

17
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:43.640
But when it comes to production, we should really be using clothing on our characters or our enemies

18
00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.640
or things that we consider to be hero objects that are going to be either on the screen a lot

19
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:53.640
or that it is really justifiable to have clothing on that particular object or character.

20
00:01:53.640 --> 00:02:01.700
So let's go ahead and pop over to the editor now and I'm going to bring the editor on the screen.

21
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.700
So this is our finished here, but we're going to go ahead and do a file a new level.

22
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.699
We're going to go to our basic and just hit create. There we go. Brand new level.

23
00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:14.699
And then what we're going to do here is right here, we're going to start importing our assets.

24
00:02:14.699 --> 00:02:22.699
So let's bring up our FBX folder here and we're just going to grab our flag FBX, bring that down right into there.

25
00:02:22.699 --> 00:02:30.949
Move this out of the way. And then for import content, we are going to be keeping it pretty much all the default.

26
00:02:30.949 --> 00:02:36.949
We're just going to hit import. And once that is done, you can see here we have our flag, our flag physics, our flag skeleton.

27
00:02:36.949 --> 00:02:40.949
We are going to need to fix this material. So let's go ahead and double click on that to open it up.

28
00:02:40.949 --> 00:02:51.300
And then we're going to take our diffuse color map here and we're just going to set this to grab this UE4 logo here and just hit apply and save.

29
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:55.300
And that's just going to give us something that will complete our material.

30
00:02:55.300 --> 00:03:00.300
And we're going to double click on the flag to open it up and bring it on over on screen so we can see here.

31
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.300
