40
00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:18.060
And we're going to do that using the gradient instead of the brush, because it's going to give us this really cool kind of soft fall off from non movable to completely movable.

41
00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.060
So we'll have something that looks very, very cloth like.

42
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:30.060
And then as it starts to get closer and closer to the end, it is going to fall off and give us something that looks kind of like this.

43
00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.060
So let's go ahead and do that now.

44
00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.060
So we're going to come back over here to the editor.

45
00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:41.060
We're going to activate our clothing paint and then we're going to select this flag cloth zero.

46
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:48.060
Automatically, our mask is going to be set to name none and the target is set to max distance.

47
00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.060
And that is what we want. We could give this a name.

48
00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.060
But for now, what we're going to be doing is we're going to click on this and we're going to go to gradient.

49
00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:02.730
And then what we're going to do is we are going to come in here and we're going to make sure we want green on this side.

50
00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:08.730
So we're just going to hold down the left mouse button as we drag down through here.

51
00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:12.730
Now, if you do make a mistake and you accidentally grab, say, something like over here, that's not a problem.

52
00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:19.730
Just hold down control and go over that and then hit control and shift and you will actually erase stuff.

53
00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:30.149
So with that, we're now going to hold control because we want the red over here and got one over there.

54
00:05:30.149 --> 00:05:36.149
So remember, control and shift. And then just to set our gradient, what we're going to do here is in our gradient in value,

55
00:05:36.149 --> 00:05:41.149
I'm going to set this to 150 and then to set the gradient in our editor, we just press the enter key.

56
00:05:41.149 --> 00:05:46.149
And that is going to set our gradient right there. And let's go ahead and close this now.

57
00:05:46.149 --> 00:05:52.149
And there we go. We have our flag falling. So it could maybe fall off a little bit better here.

58
00:05:52.149 --> 00:05:55.149
But for right now, that is some pretty good results.

59
00:05:55.149 --> 00:06:00.149
So the other cool thing that we could do with our cloth is actually immediately test this in a level.

60
00:06:00.149 --> 00:06:03.149
So I'm just going to pull this off screen here.

61
00:06:03.149 --> 00:06:06.149
And what we're going to do is we're going to look for a wind source directional action.

62
00:06:06.149 --> 00:06:12.149
We're just going to put that in to our level. And then as soon as we do that, we can actually see the cloth being affected by this.

63
00:06:12.149 --> 00:06:21.149
So let's go back over here to unreal. We're going to grab our skeletal mesh because we need that to operate on.

64
00:06:21.149 --> 00:06:27.860
So there we go. And then we're going to look for our wind. There's our wind directional source.

65
00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:30.860
And we're just going to set this to like a value of five and five.

66
00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:34.860
And we do need to hit alt s or simulate to get this to work.

67
00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.860
And then if we grab that wind directional source, we kind of like move it around in here.

68
00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:47.339
You can see here it is going to be affecting that. We could maybe set set some of this stuff up a little bit higher.

69
00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:50.339
There we go. 15 is giving us some some pretty crazy results.

70
00:06:50.339 --> 00:06:57.339
So we might want to do it that high. But you can see here we're getting those results automatically without having to do anything else.

71
00:06:57.339 --> 00:07:05.339
Just simply enable the cloth and then drop it right in and it will work on whatever you happen to be utilizing.

72
00:07:05.339 --> 00:07:09.620
So while this is very, very cool, go ahead and stop this.

73
00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:16.620
There is one other thing that we need to do to make cloth run efficiently, and that is add level of details.

74
00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:23.300
Now, level of details are going to be required no matter what version of clothing you are using.

75
00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.300
And there are a couple of ways that you can create them.

76
00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:35.300
We can have the editor automatically create the level of details for us, or we can create the level of details in our DCC and bring them over manually.

77
00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:38.300
And that's what we're doing in this particular case.

78
00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:46.300
In our case, what we're going to be doing now is we're going to be importing our premade level of detail one, two and three.

79
00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:50.300
This is you can see here. So this is our base mesh and then one, two and three.

80
00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.300
And it's just simply fewer triangles in there.

81
00:07:53.300 --> 00:08:00.300
And to do this, what we're going to do first is we are going to go in to our LOD settings.

82
00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:04.300
We're going to do LOD import. We're going to select it.

83
00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:13.300
Import LOD one. Grab our LOD one FBX and then we are going to create clothing LOD from selection.

84
00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:20.459
Make sure that we remap our parent parameters and then we will add our level of detail one.

85
00:08:20.459 --> 00:08:26.970
And then we will apply our level of detail one. And if this sounds a little complicated, that's OK.

86
00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:33.059
We're going to go through this step by step now because the steps, while there are a few of them, are actually repeatable.

87
00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:37.059
And it's pretty simple once we've done it a few times.

88
00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:44.059
So, again, we're going to come over here to our LOD settings and we're going to go import LOD level one.

89
00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:48.159
This is going to then prompt us to select the level of detail we wish to import.

90
00:08:48.159 --> 00:08:51.610
So we're going to hit this one and hit open. All right.

91
00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:57.610
And then what we're going to do is we're going to right click because we see that successfully import.

92
00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:00.610
And we need to actually first come up here and we're going to go to LOD one.

93
00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:02.610
And you should see we actually don't see anything here.

94
00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:12.379
And that's because we need to right click on this and we need to create clothing data from create clothing LOD from selection.

95
00:09:12.379 --> 00:09:14.379
And we're going to create it from here.

96
00:09:14.379 --> 00:09:23.169
We're going to add LOD one, remap parameters and then hit create.

97
00:09:23.169 --> 00:09:31.169
All right. And once that's been created, we're going to apply clothing data and we're going to apply clothing data LOD one.

98
00:09:31.169 --> 00:09:36.289
And then now we've got LOD zero and LOD one.

99
00:09:36.289 --> 00:09:46.639
So, again, what we're going to do here is we're going to come to our LOD import, import level of detail to select our second level of detail and press open.

100
00:09:46.639 --> 00:09:51.500
And then that's going to give us our second level of detail here.

101
00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:56.600
So we're going to right click on that and we're going to go create clothing LOD from selection.

102
00:09:56.600 --> 00:10:03.600
Well, we're going to target asset is the first level of detail and we are going to add a second level of detail.

103
00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.600
So we have the base match level of detail one level of detail two.

104
00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:10.600
We're going to make sure to remap our parameters and then hit create.

105
00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:19.100
And then what we're going to do is apply our level of detail to.

106
00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:22.100
OK, fantastic. Then we're going to do this one more time.

107
00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:27.480
Import level of detail three. We're going to select our third level of detail.

108
00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.399
Press open. Give us a second to import. There we go.

109
00:10:32.399 --> 00:10:37.720
OK, click it down to level of three here. So now we're actually looking at the third level.

110
00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:41.720
Create clothing LOD from section. What do we want to do?

111
00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:47.940
The target asset, again, is going to be the first level of not the first level of detail, but the main mesh.

112
00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:57.419
We are going to add a third level of detail, make sure that we remap parameters and press create.

113
00:10:57.419 --> 00:11:03.279
Then we are going to apply that third level of detail clothing.

114
00:11:03.279 --> 00:11:09.470
And now what we should have here is zero one, two and three.

115
00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:14.470
And what that remap is doing is it's basically making sure that as we go down the different level of details,

116
00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:22.730
notice that our gradient is still staying there, even though we are missing quite a bit of information.

117
00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:30.730
Basically, what is happening is that remap is automatically applying whatever it is we had in the previous section

118
00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:36.730
to map what is below it, saving us time so that we don't have to go in and paint every single section.

119
00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:41.730
And what this is going to give us inside of unreal is now we're going to have our base.

120
00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:48.730
And then as we start to move further and further away from the camera, we're going to get our LOD zero, LOD two, LOD three.

121
00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.730
So let's go ahead and just pop inside of the editor now.

122
00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:57.730
So I'm going to close this. And if we just hit Alt S, we should see this start to simulate.

123
00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:00.730
We're going to see here it is actually doing its LOD.

124
00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:06.730
But since it is such a good representation of it, it's actually kind of hard to see it move.

125
00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:14.940
So what we're going to do here is we can come down to our not our landscape.

126
00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:19.940
We want our optimization. That's the wrong one right there.

127
00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:23.940
Not our visibility. We want our optimization. And I would like to see.

128
00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:27.940
No, that's not the one that I'm looking for. I am looking for.

129
00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:33.419
Let's just go back to lit mode really quick. We want level of detail.

130
00:12:33.419 --> 00:12:36.419
There it is. And we want our mesh LOD coloration.

131
00:12:36.419 --> 00:12:41.419
So this is going to be base. And then as we start to get away, it's going to start to change colors.

132
00:12:41.419 --> 00:12:51.610
So we go from this to LOD one, LOD two and then finally LOD three.

133
00:12:51.610 --> 00:13:06.669
The last part of the chaos clothing quick start is going to talk about where you can find different settings to adjust the various properties of how your cloth is going to react.

134
00:13:06.669 --> 00:13:10.669
This is both reacted to the clothing itself as well as the environment.

135
00:13:10.669 --> 00:13:15.669
So under your clothing, you're going to find these cloth configs.

136
00:13:15.669 --> 00:13:19.669
There's the chaos clothing configure. I'm sorry, chaos cloth config.

137
00:13:19.669 --> 00:13:24.669
And then the chaos cloth shared SIM config. This bottom one right here.

138
00:13:24.669 --> 00:13:34.669
We're going to go over this first because this is a little bit shorter and it will affect how chaos is simulated through your entire project.

139
00:13:34.669 --> 00:13:40.669
So basically the simulation here, the iteration count is one, the maximum iteration count.

140
00:13:40.669 --> 00:13:50.090
And then if we have any in between subdivision iteration counts for sub stepping our physics,

141
00:13:50.090 --> 00:14:04.090
I would highly recommend that you leave these at the default setting unless you have done significant performance testing to ensure that increasing these numbers or adjusting these numbers are affecting your performance.

142
00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:14.090
Basically right now it is set at, well, between one and ten iterations and that is going to give us the results that you are seeing.

143
00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:17.090
And they're actually fairly, fairly good for default values.

144
00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:26.090
Now, if your project is favoring heavier cloth usage and you want to increase the iteration count to two or three,

145
00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:28.090
well, that's totally possible here.

146
00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:38.090
You need to make sure that you have properly tested the performance implications of that because that is quite a big increase in the iteration count.

147
00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:47.090
And remember, iteration is going to deal with how lifelike or how realistic your cloth simulation is going to look.

148
00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:50.090
The next part is the chaos clothing config.

149
00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:59.090
And these right here affect how the material is going to react if it's going to behave like silk or burlap or something of that nature,

150
00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:05.090
as well as how it is going to react to things like the environment and collision.

151
00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:08.090
What happens if it stretches?

152
00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:16.090
If we pop over to the editor really quick, we're not going to go through all of these because the coolest thing about these values is we can actually test all of them.

153
00:15:16.090 --> 00:15:20.250
We deactivate this. There we go.

154
00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:28.250
We come in here, we can change something like the density here and it'll reactivate the simulation as we adjust the different parameters.

155
00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:34.250
Things here, too, are also going to be much smaller values than you might be used to.

156
00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:39.250
You might be used to doing something like maybe like just do 0.125.

157
00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:44.250
Right. And that might not give you the you know, maybe you do like one to six.

158
00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:55.250
In this case, doing things like adding more value like this, maybe we divide by 0.2 or multiply by 0.225.

159
00:15:55.250 --> 00:16:03.379
Adding and removing values in this way is really going to help make sure that you get the best values possible.

160
00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:07.600
Also, there are a lot of different values here that you can adjust.

161
00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:18.789
So don't be afraid to experiment and turn things on, turn things off, adjust and see if they give you the values that you need.

162
00:16:18.789 --> 00:16:28.019
Remember, if you ever want to get back to any default values, all you need to do is simply reset this property to the default and it will reset there.

163
00:16:28.019 --> 00:16:30.019
Other things to look at here.

164
00:16:30.019 --> 00:16:32.019
Collision. This is really important.

165
00:16:32.019 --> 00:16:39.019
Use continuous collision detection if we want to make sure that we have something that is always checking for collision,

166
00:16:39.019 --> 00:16:48.049
because maybe it's inner penetrating something and we don't want that inner penetration to happen using self collisions.

167
00:16:48.049 --> 00:16:55.940
This is detecting whether or not we are colliding with ourselves and then reactions with our animation properties,

168
00:16:55.940 --> 00:17:03.940
you know, to ensure that we are not getting too crazy on the cloth simulation if we are attached to something that is moving.

169
00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:08.940
So there's a lot of really great properties that we can adjust in here per piece of cloth that we have.

170
00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:16.940
So it's really important that we know about those properties and where we can adjust them so that we can get the most out of our simulations.
